as the same claim as e287, e385; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e303 [fact; TIKR; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] Nvidia reported $75 billion in Data Center revenue in its recent quarter, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e287, e331, e375; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e333 [party_statement; barchart.com (author); original source Nvidia management ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] Nvidia guided to approximately $91 billion in Q2 FY2027 revenue, assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\\n    Quote: Management also issued strong guidance, forecasting approximately $91 billion in Q2 revenue despite assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e291, e380, e396; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e341 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:635f19578778394086009f6f 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $75.2 billion in its most recent fiscal quarter, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data center revenue of $75.2 billion climbed 92%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e331, e384; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e375 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3a9054a950c410e249837c91 2026-07-13] Nvidia's Data Center segment revenue was a record $75 billion in FY2027 Q1, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: The results were driven by record data center revenue of $75 billion, up 92%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e303; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e377 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion, up 143% year over year.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q2 FY2026 revenue hit $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%.\",\n   \"- e378 [party_statement; Alex Sirois; original source Hock Tan, Broadcom CEO ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan attributed the company’s sales mix to increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking.\\n    Quote: CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being “driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking.”\",\n   \"- e379 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.23%, and Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion; networking revenue was up 199%.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s Q1 FY2027 revenue reached $81.615 billion, up 85.23%, with Data Center at $75.246 billion and networking alone up 199%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e115; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e380 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois; original source Alex Sirois 2026-07-13; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] The author said NVIDIA’s Q2 guidance assumed zero China Data Center compute revenue.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA leans on scale and CUDA gravity, but assumes zero China Data Center compute revenue in Q2 guidance, a headwind Broadcom does not carry to the same degree.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e333; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e381 [party_statement; Alex Sirois; original source Broadcom management ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom management guided to $16.0 billion of AI revenue for Q3, roughly 54% of total sales.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q3 earnings report lands September 8, and management has already telegraphed $16.0 billion in AI revenue, roughly 54% of total sales.\",\n   \"- e383 [fact; Vandita Jadeja, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:301b0738dab6ca32e88b019a 2026-07-13] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, including Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion.\\n    Quote: Q1 FY27 revenue hit $81.61 billion, +85.2% YoY, with Data Center at $75.25 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e293; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e384 [fact; 247wallst.com, citing NVIDIA’s reported results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $75.246 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion , up 92% YoY.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e341; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e385 [fact; 247wallst.com, citing NVIDIA’s reported results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA Data Center networking revenue was $14.8 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center networking alone did $14.8 billion, up 199%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e294; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e387 [fact; 247wallst.com, citing AMD’s reported results; original source AMD ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] AMD reported $5.775 billion in Data Center revenue in Q1 2026.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 data center revenue of $5.775 billion.\",\n   \"- e393 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92%, and Networking revenue growth of 199%, for the quarter discussed.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion, up 92%, with Networking exploding 199%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e287; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e398 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source Milk Road AI ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Milk Road AI estimated that one gigawatt of AI infrastructure costs $6–11 billion with Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks, about $19 billion with Nvidia GB300 racks, and $25 billion under Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks. Under Vera Rubin, rack costs jump to $25 billion.\",\n   \"- e399 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley’s forecast, as reported by the author, put the four hyperscalers’ 2027 AI-infrastructure spending above $1 trillion and incremental compute capacity at 19.5 GW; Google was forecast to add 6.8 GW, compared with about 6.7 GW added by the four in 2025.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\",\n   \"- e301 [fact; Thomas Richmond, citing NVIDIA’s report; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e226, e418; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e333 [party_statement; barchart.com (author); original source Nvidia management ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] Nvidia guided to approximately $91 billion in Q2 FY2027 revenue, assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\\n    Quote: Management also issued strong guidance, forecasting approximately $91 billion in Q2 revenue despite assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e291, e380, e396, e437; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e384 [fact; 247wallst.com, citing NVIDIA’s reported results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $75.246 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion , up 92% YoY.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e341, e433; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e385 [fact; 247wallst.com, citing NVIDIA’s reported results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA Data Center networking revenue was $14.8 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center networking alone did $14.8 billion, up 199%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e294, e411, e419, e434; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e408 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7 billion.\\n    Quote: For comparison, Nvidia's data center revenue in fiscal 2026 (which ended in January this year) was $193.7 billion.\",\n   \"- e409 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA generated $162.3 billion of fiscal 2026 Data Center revenue from compute chips; the remaining Data Center revenue came from networking components.\\n    Quote: It is worth noting that $162.3 billion of its fiscal 2026 data center revenue came from sales of compute chips, while the rest was from networking components.\",\n   \"- e410 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2026 networking revenue of $31.4 billion, up 142% year over year.\\n    Quote: The company reported a 142% year-over-year increase in networking revenue in fiscal 2026 to $31.4 billion.\",\n   \"- e411 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA's networking revenue tripled year over year to $14.8 billion in the opening quarter of fiscal 2027.\\n    Quote: networking revenue tripling year-over-year to $14.8 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e385; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e419 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] NVIDIA’s Data Center Networking revenue was $14.8 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: with Data Center Networking alone growing 199% YoY to $14.8 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e385, e428; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e421 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Third-party estimates ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Third-party estimates put Nvidia's share of the AI chip market at 80%; the period is not specified.\\n    Quote: The good news is that Nvidia still controls an impressive 80% of the AI chip market, according to third-party estimates.\",\n   \"- e422 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-14; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] The author says the shift toward inference and competing chip technologies are weighing on Nvidia's stock performance; the period is 2026.\\n    Quote: The shift from AI training to inference and the emergence of competing chip technologies explain why Nvidia stock has risen just 8% this year despite sustaining impressive growth levels.\",\n   \"- e423 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-14; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] The author says inference requires less computing power than training, a shift that could reduce demand for Nvidia's data center products; no period is specified.\\n    Quote: As a result, the inference phase doesn't require as much computing power as the training phase.\",\n   \"- e424 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-14; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] The author estimates Marvell controls 20% to 25% of the custom AI processor market; the period is not specified.\\n    Quote: Marvell is one of the key players in custom AI processors, controlling 20% to 25% of this market.\",\n   \"- e425 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it has more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, with potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion; no completion period is specified.\\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\",\n   \"- e428 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA’s Data Center Networking revenue grew 199% year over year in Q1 FY2027.\\n    Quote: with Data Center Networking alone growing 199% on InfiniBand, NVLink, and Spectrum-X pull-through.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e419; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e433 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: with Data Center alone contributing $75.246 billion (+92% YoY).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e384, e439; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e434 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Networking revenue of $14.8 billion, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Networking was the sleeper hit at $14.8 billion (+199% YoY), driven by InfiniBand, Spectrum-X, and NVLink.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e385; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e439 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center segment revenue of $75.25 billion, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center at $75.25B (+92%).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e433; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e440 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Broadcom management ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] Broadcom guided to Q3 revenue of $29.4 billion, up 84% year over year, and stated a goal of exceeding $100 billion in AI sales by 2027.\\n    Quote: Broadcom guided Q3 revenue to $29.4B (+84% YoY), and management’s stated goal is to exceed $100B in AI sales by 2027.\",\n   \"- e441 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Broadcom ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] Broadcom reported AI semiconductor revenue of $10.8 billion last quarter, up 143% year over year.\\n    Quote: AI semi revenue grew 143% YoY to $10.8B last quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e342; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e451 [fact; Tom's Hardware, reporting Reuters; original source U.S. government 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] The U.S. approved ZTE and Maginfra to buy Nvidia H200 chips on July 14, 2026.\\n    Quote: Today, Reuters reports that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra have received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-gen H200 \\\"Hopper\\\" chips.\",\n   \"- e452 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] U.S. policy allows case-by-case sales of Nvidia Hopper-family chips, but not Blackwell chips, to Chinese firms and imposes a 25% export tariff.\\n    Quote: The current status of the AI chip trade situation is roughly that the U.S. allows Chinese firms to buy AI chips up to and including the Hopper family (meaning no Blackwell chips), with a 25% export tariff, though final decisions are made on a case-by-case basis.\",\n   \"- e453 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware, citing CNBC; original source U.S. trade official 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official said very few shipments had taken place against licenses for H200 chips and equivalents as of July 14, 2026.\\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \\\"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It’s a very small quantity of chips\\\" during a congressional hearing.\",\n   \"- e458 [fact; mlq.ai; original source FBI ; from gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402 2026-07-14] The FBI's RFI specifies a single-node training-server category with one Nvidia HGX B300 eight-GPU assembly and Nvidia BlueField-3 data processing units; the RFI does not commit the FBI to a procurement.\\n    Quote: Category 1 covers single-node training servers built around Intel Xeon 6767P processors with 2-4 TB of RAM, one Nvidia HGX B300 eight-GPU assembly, SSD storage, 25Gb Ethernet, and Nvidia BlueField-3 data processing units for network offload and security [1] .\",\n   \"- e459 [fact; mlq.ai; original source FBI ; from gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402 2026-07-14] The FBI's RFI specifies an integrated rack-scale system category requiring one GB300 NVL72 equivalent with high-speed interconnect, Spectrum-X networking and in-rack cooling; the RFI does not commit the FBI to a procurement.\\n    Quote: Category 2 steps up to integrated rack-scale systems, specifying one GB300 NVL72 equivalent — Nvidia's flagship 72-GPU liquid-cooled rack — along with high-speed interconnect fabric, Spectrum-X networking architecture, and in-rack cooling [1] .\",\n   \"- e460 [fact; mlq.ai; original source FBI ; from gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402 2026-07-14] The FBI's RFI specifies an AI pod category with performance matching five GB300 NVL72 systems, while allowing Google TPU v8 or v7L or better; the RFI does not commit the FBI to a procurement.\\n    Quote: Category 3 is the most ambitious: an AI pod architecture equivalent to Google TPU v8 or v7L, or better, with performance matching five GB300 NVL72 systems.\",\n   \"- e461 [fact; mlq.ai; original source FBI ; from gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402 2026-07-14] The FBI's RFI specifies Nvidia L40-S accelerators or equivalent for an inference category, with 48GB of GPU memory; the RFI does not commit the FBI to a procurement.\\n    Quote: Category 4 targets inference workloads specifically, calling for Nvidia L40-S accelerators or equivalent with 48GB of GPU memory and support for FP32, FP16, BF16, and INT8 precision formats [1] .\",\n   \"- e462 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source mlq.ai 2026-07-14; from gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402 2026-07-14] Federal AI spending obligations reached $7.2 billion in calendar 2026, compared with $675 million in 2024, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Federal AI spending obligations reached $7.2 billion in 2026, up from $675 million in 2024 — a 966% increase.\"\n  ],\n  \"points_to_rule\": [\n   \"item i295\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 21,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v3\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that first shipments of its Vera Rubin AI chip platform to customers have begun by December 31, 2026, through a press release, earnings call, or quarterly filing?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA confirms that first shipments of Vera Rubin to customers have begun by December 31, 2026.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA formally announces after July 2, 2026, that it has canceled or abandoned the Vera Rubin platform before any first shipment to a customer. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i105\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin systems in full production; shipping to eight cloud partners including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall (2026)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened (full production); plan (shipping this fall)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i219\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n    \"passage\": \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 first shipments and volume ramp\",\n     \"period\": \"first shipments Q3 2026; volume ramp Q4 2026\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i202\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Tom's Hardware\",\n     \"what\": \"scope of the reported delay: Rubin Ultra/Kyber only; 2026 Rubin on Oberon unaffected\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i207\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (as restated)\",\n     \"what\": \"Rubin systems in full production; first shipments to eight cloud partners incl. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\",\n     \"status_note\": \"in full production; shipping this fall is a plan\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e40 [fact; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] In an article dated July 2, 2026, Fool.com reported that NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later in 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source Jensen Huang / NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has stated expectations for $1 trillion in orders for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027, according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source Jensen Huang / NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has stated expectations for $1 trillion in orders for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027, according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e72; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e72 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, Fool.com; original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said in March 2026 that he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027, as reported by Fool.com.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e81 [party_statement; Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability at NVIDIA (via bgr.com article by Jonathan Sayers); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a 2026-07-04] NVIDIA, in a company blog post by Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability, announced that NVIDIA DSX AI factories are hosting NVIDIA's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles, using a 100% liquid-cooling design in which coolant enters the chip at 45°C and exits at 55°C.\\n    Quote: In Nvidia's blog post by Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability at Nvidia, it was announced that Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles. … Liquid coolant enters the chip at 45 degrees Celsius, absorbs heat from across the chip's surface, and exits at 55 degrees.\",\n   \"- e40 [fact; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] In an article dated July 2, 2026, Fool.com reported that NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later in 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e93 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e97 [fact; Adria Cimino, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia is launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform, per the article's account of Nvidia's plan.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\",\n   \"- e99 [party_statement; Adria Cimino, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\",\n   \"- e40 [fact; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] In an article dated July 2, 2026, Fool.com reported that NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later in 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93, e172; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e119 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are reported to have first shipments scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the article's account of the launch schedule.\\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n   \"- e120 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source  ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into their upcoming products.\\n    Quote: Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI build\",\n   \"- e134 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source  ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e168 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, via Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted SemiAnalysis's claims that its next-generation rack architectures are delayed, saying: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e166 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"- e170 [party_statement; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein, reported by Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (management) ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Hon Hai Precision Industry, one of Nvidia's largest suppliers, reported strong earnings on the Friday before the 2026-07-06 article and its management forecast a continued acceleration in AI demand; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein called this \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\" No figures for the results were given.\\n    Quote: The analyst additionally pointed to strong earnings last Friday from Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (TW:2317), one of Nvidia's largest suppliers. Klein noted that Hon Hai management forecasted a continued acceleration in AI demand, which the analyst said was \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\"\",\n   \"- e172 [party_statement; tikr.com (TIKR); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud, according to the article.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e180 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA representative told Tom's Hardware, in a statement dated 7/6/2026, that \\\"Our roadmap is intact,\\\" providing no further details in response to follow-up questions about the reported Kyber delay.\\n    Quote: Update 7/6/2026 4:10pm PT : An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e182 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source  ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom's Hardware reported that the delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\\n    Quote: The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n   \"- e195 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] Nvidia disputed a SemiAnalysis report that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays, according to CNBC; the item reports the dispute occurred on the Monday before the 2026-07-07 article and does not quote Nvidia's statement or give any revised schedule. Nvidia's denial is the company's own assertion that its product plan/schedule stands.\\n    Quote: Similar action took place in Nvidia Monday after the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e182 (); both are in force: Tom's Hardware reported that the delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\",\n   \"- e237 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Nvidia is working to release its next-generation Rubin AI chip line-up in the second half of 2026, following the Blackwell generation introduced in late 2024, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\",\n   \"- e284 [third_party_view; Bruno Ferreira, Tom's Hardware (citing Phoronix); original source Phoronix ; from gnews:9fbd0f954b9cc2281da531d1 2026-07-08] Phoronix test-drove one of Nvidia's 'upcoming' Arm-based Vera CPUs and reported strong single-threaded performance, favorably comparing it with Intel Xeon and AMD Epyc x86 processors in specific single-threaded workloads. The chip is described as upcoming, indicating it was in pre-launch testing rather than shipping.\\n    Quote: Only a little while back, Phoronix got the chance to test-drive one of Nvidia's upcoming Arm-based Vera CPUs. In certain approved workloads, the chip put up an impressive showing , nipping at the heels of its Xeon and Epyc x86 competitors. In specific single-threaded scenarios, Vera \\\"absolutely dusted the competition\\\" (our words).\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source Jensen Huang / NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has stated expectations for $1 trillion in orders for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027, according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e72, e312; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e306 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia denied speculation that its Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure is delayed, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track; the article states Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system.\\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e339; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e134 (); both are in force: NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\",\n   \"- e312 [party_statement; Adam Spatacco, Fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management has articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers, as reported by Fool.com on July 9, 2026.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e339 [party_statement; Bloomberg (via moomoo); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Nvidia issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay and confirming that its product roadmap remains intact, a clarification the article says counters concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt its AI competitive lead.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e306; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e251 (); both are in force: Research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks; the article says this could push NVIDIA to use its new credit/revenue-sharing model to sustain demand.\",\n   \"- e99 [party_statement; Adria Cimino, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e382; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e365 [fact; quantumzeitgeist.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-07; from gnews:b666d35e5247a8ddcc2d7c72 2026-07-10] NVIDIA unveiled Vera, a new data center CPU architected for agentic AI workloads that prioritizes single-threaded (per-core) performance rather than the industry's trend toward higher core counts at the expense of per-core speed. This is a product unveiling, not a confirmed shipment or revenue event.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is challenging a core tenet of modern data center design with Vera, a new CPU built to maximize single-threaded performance at scale, and AI innovator Perplexity is among the first to adopt it. … The newly unveiled NVIDIA Vera CPU is specifically architected to address the demands of agentic AI, a field where maximizing single-threaded performance is paramount\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e386; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e366 [fact; quantumzeitgeist.com; original source Perplexity 2026-07-07; from gnews:b666d35e5247a8ddcc2d7c72 2026-07-10] Perplexity, an AI company developing agentic systems, is among the first to adopt NVIDIA's Vera CPU; after testing it on real coding workflows, Perplexity is planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems.\\n    Quote: Perplexity, a company actively developing agentic systems, is among the first to adopt the NVIDIA Vera CPU, signaling a significant endorsement of the new architecture’s capabilities. … Perplexity is now planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems, demonstrating confidence in its ability to enhance agent performance and overall system efficiency.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e188; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e306 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia denied speculation that its Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure is delayed, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track; the article states Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system.\\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e339, e389; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e134 (); both are in force: NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\",\n   \"- e407 [fact; I/O Fund (io-fund.com article); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2dcfaef1a91c2976a4e347da 2026-07-12] NVIDIA has developed its CMX Context Memory Storage Platform, an SSD-based enclosure connected to Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs over Spectrum-X Ethernet, using 64 BlueField-4 DPUs per rack connected to roughly 9,600 TB of SSD storage capacity; NVIDIA also created the STX reference architecture, a standardized blueprint for storage vendors to build CMX systems that connect to Vera Rubin racks. The I/O Fund reports this as an NVIDIA platform and potential new revenue driver.\\n    Quote: To solve for KV cache bottlenecks, Nvidia has developed its CMX Context Memory Storage Platform. CMX uses an SSD-based enclosure that is connected to Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs over Spectrum-X Ethernet. … The hardware offload engine in CMX is the BlueField-4 DPUs. CMX incorporates 64 BlueField-4 DPUs, which connect to approximately 9,600 TB of SSD storage capacity per rack. … Nvidia also created the\",\n   \"- e412 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA (Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] At Nvidia's latest product presentation, CEO Jensen Huang introduced the Vera CPU, a processor designed specifically for AI workloads and agentic AI systems; management says Vera works alongside Nvidia's AI chips and delivers roughly 1.8 times the performance of comparable x86 processors.\\n    Quote: During the company's latest product presentation, CEO Jensen Huang introduced the Vera CPU, a processor designed specifically for AI workloads and agentic AI systems. … Management says Vera delivers roughly 1.8 times the performance of comparable x86 processors while working alongside Nvidia's AI chips.\",\n   \"- e413 [rumour; barchart.com (citing unnamed reports); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] According to unnamed reports, Nvidia has begun offering its new Vera processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow. The item frames this as 'reports suggest,' not a confirmed shipment or order.\\n    Quote: Reports also suggest Nvidia has begun offering the new processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow.\",\n   \"- e428 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform (reported among its recent product and capability expansions); the item describes it as a launch, not as confirmed customer shipments.\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, released the AI PC platform RTX Spark, expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL), and further invested in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\",\n   \"- e429 [rumour; en.sedaily.com, citing unnamed semiconductor-industry sources; original source LG Electronics 2026-07-12; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development in the first half of 2026 of an AI server-rack prototype built to the compute-tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" platform, with the R&D done at a 1.8-megawatt data center cluster at its Pyeongtaek site; LG plans reliability evaluations in the second half and then to secure orders from global big tech companies and begin mass production next year (2027). The report is attributed to unnamed semiconductor-industry sources; it does not state any order, volume or revenue.\\n    Quote: LG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year. … According to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" in the first half of the year. LG plans t\",\n   \"- e434 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] In investor-relations discussions, NVIDIA said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\\n    Quote: The company said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e134 (); both are in force: NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\",\n   \"- e237 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Nvidia is working to release its next-generation Rubin AI chip line-up in the second half of 2026, following the Blackwell generation introduced in late 2024, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e534; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e486 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] According to a report about 21 days before the 2026-07-14 article, South Korea's SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above those on HBM; the article characterizes this as a margin story rather than a demand story.\\n    Quote: The previous big move we wrote about was 21 days ago when the stock dropped 3.7% after a report that South Korea's SK Hynix is slowing its high-bandwidth memory (HBM) expansion rattled the AI-chip complex. … The headline sounds bearish for AI, but the underlying report is a margin story, not a demand story. SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM,\",\n   \"- e506 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] The article states that NVIDIA's Blackwell 300 is ramping and that the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it, giving no shipping date for Vera Rubin. It is the author's description of the product roadmap rather than a new NVIDIA announcement.\\n    Quote: Blackwell 300 is ramping, and the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it.\",\n   \"- e534 [party_statement; EBC (ebc.com); original source NVIDIA Corp. ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Nvidia's Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, per EBC's report; an analyst cited in the article expects the August earnings report to be a catalyst on margins.\\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e237; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e541 [fact; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Under an eight-year strategic partnership between Firmus Technologies and Nvidia aimed at expanding AI infrastructure across the region, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation GPUs in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies. The plan dates the customer deployment, not confirmed Nvidia shipments or revenue.\\n    Quote: The Batam campus is also part of an eight-year strategic partnership between Firmus Technologies and Nvidia aimed at expanding AI infrastructure across the region. … Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and f\"\n  ],\n  \"points_to_rule\": [\n   \"same_question\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 26,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v2\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA make revenue-generating first shipments of its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026, as confirmed by an official NVIDIA shipment announcement or company filing?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Whether Vera Rubin begins shipping in 2026, as the item reports it 'could begin shipping later this year'.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA makes revenue-generating first shipments of its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026, confirmed by an official NVIDIA shipment announcement or company filing.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"condition\": \"After July 2, 2026, NVIDIA formally announces that it has abandoned Vera Rubin or postponed its first shipments until after 2026. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i105\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin systems in full production; shipping to eight cloud partners including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall (2026)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened (full production); plan (shipping this fall)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i219\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n    \"passage\": \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 first shipments and volume ramp\",\n     \"period\": \"first shipments Q3 2026; volume ramp Q4 2026\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i202\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Tom's Hardware\",\n     \"what\": \"scope of the reported delay: Rubin Ultra/Kyber only; 2026 Rubin on Oberon unaffected\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i207\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (as restated)\",\n     \"what\": \"Rubin systems in full production; first shipments to eight cloud partners incl. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\",\n     \"status_note\": \"in full production; shipping this fall is a plan\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e40 [party_statement; Justin Pope, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year (2026), per the article.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e75; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e75 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said in March that he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time (March 2026) and the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e94 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n   \"- e98 [fact; Adria Cimino (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA is launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU in fall 2026 as part of its Vera Rubin platform, per the article — its first move into the CPU market. The item reports a launch plan/date, not shipments or revenue.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\",\n   \"- e99 [party_statement; Adria Cimino (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in 2026 (\\\"this year\\\"), per the article, as it prepares to launch its first stand-alone CPU in fall 2026. This is company guidance, not reported revenue.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\",\n   \"- e94 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e121, e179; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e119 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, according to the bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale. The bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. … The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n   \"- e121 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley says first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e138 [rumour; 24/7 Wall St. (Danielle Liverance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across the company's upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems, according to 24/7 Wall St.; the report does not identify the source of the delay reports, name which Vera Rubin products are affected, or give a new timing.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e173 [party_statement; Investing.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted the reported product-roadmap delays, saying: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e170 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, leaving NVIDIA without a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now — a gap that could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in.\",\n   \"- e179 [fact; tikr.com (TIKR); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and are set to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to the article.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e188 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Tom's Hardware (Luke James) ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom's Hardware reported that the Kyber delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack, and that NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\\n    Quote: The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n   \"- e40 [party_statement; Justin Pope, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year (2026), per the article.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e242; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e242 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] NVIDIA is working to release its more powerful Rubin AI chip line-up in the second half of calendar 2026, the article reports.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e75, e308; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e302 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] NVIDIA denied reports that Kyber could be delayed, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. It said Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key part of its long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e256 (); both are in force: Research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks; the item says this could push NVIDIA to lean further on its new credit and revenue-sharing model to sustain demand.\",\n   \"- e334 [party_statement; moomoo; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] NVIDIA issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay and confirming that its product roadmap remains intact.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e99 [party_statement; Adria Cimino (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in 2026 (\\\"this year\\\"), per the article, as it prepares to launch its first stand-alone CPU in fall 2026. This is company guidance, not reported revenue.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e378; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e411 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] At NVIDIA's latest product presentation, CEO Jensen Huang introduced the Vera CPU, a processor designed for AI workloads and agentic AI systems.\\n    Quote: During the company's latest product presentation, CEO Jensen Huang introduced the Vera CPU, a processor designed specifically for AI workloads and agentic AI systems.\",\n   \"- e412 [rumour; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] Reports suggest NVIDIA has begun offering its new Vera processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow.\\n    Quote: Reports also suggest Nvidia has begun offering the new processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow.\",\n   \"- e425 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] The article states that NVIDIA recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform (alongside releasing the RTX Spark AI PC platform); it gives no launch date, shipment timing or volume.\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, released the AI PC platform RTX Spark, expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL), and further invested in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\",\n   \"- e426 [rumour; en.sedaily.com (Lee Seok-jin), citing unnamed semiconductor-industry sources; original source LG Electronics 2026-07-12; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute-tray specifications of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform in the first half of 2026; LG plans to secure orders from global big-tech customers after reliability evaluations in the second half and to begin mass production of the AI server rack next year (2027). The article attributes the account to unnamed industry sources.\\n    Quote: LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" in the first half of the year. LG plans to secure orders from global big tech companies after reliability evaluations in the second half and begin mass production next year.\",\n   \"- e542 [party_statement; EBC (ebc.com); original source NVIDIA Corp. ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Nvidia's Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping to target shipping dates fast, and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026.\\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\"\n  ],\n  \"points_to_rule\": [\n   \"same_question\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 28,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Rubin Ultra generation, in Kyber NVL144 racks, by December 31, 2027?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA make first shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack with Vera Rubin Ultra during calendar 2027, as determined by NVIDIA shipment confirmations, earnings-call disclosures, or independently confirmed customer deployments?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Whether the Kyber rack debuts and ships with Vera Rubin Ultra in calendar 2027 (consistent with NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact') or is delayed to 2028 (SemiAnalysis), and when first shipments actually occur.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA makes first shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack with Vera Rubin Ultra by 2027-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"condition\": \"After 2026-07-05, NVIDIA announces that it has canceled Kyber or formally deferred its first shipments with Vera Rubin Ultra until 2028. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i100\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n     \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n     \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n     \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i179\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n    \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\",\n     \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\",\n     \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i181\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\",\n     \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\",\n     \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i101\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i197\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n     \"what\": \"denies the report: roadmap intact\",\n     \"condition\": \"unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e91 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post published Monday that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, which is designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; the larger NVL576 system linking eight racks via optical connections is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the syst\",\n   \"- e92 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its next-generation Kyber rack system is delayed, stating that 'Our roadmap is intact.'\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n   \"- e93 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that a backup plan to bolt two of NVIDIA's current-generation racks together to reach similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra'; SemiAnalysis said this could give Advanced Micro Devices and Google a technical opening at the high end of the market.\\n    Quote: A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said. … That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no pro\",\n   \"- e91 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post published Monday that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, which is designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; the larger NVL576 system linking eight racks via optical connections is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the syst\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e107, e125, e126, e167, e172, e176, e182, e184; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e92 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its next-generation Kyber rack system is delayed, stating that 'Our roadmap is intact.'\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e109, e129, e173, e189; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e93 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that a backup plan to bolt two of NVIDIA's current-generation racks together to reach similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra'; SemiAnalysis said this could give Advanced Micro Devices and Google a technical opening at the high end of the market.\\n    Quote: A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said. … That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no pro\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e108, e168, e174, e178, e185; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e107 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com (beincrypto.com original by Lockridge Okoth); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis alleges that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 high-density rack architecture, built for Rubin Ultra GPUs, has slipped by more than 12 months, blaming persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\\n    Quote: The clash pits Nvidia against research firm SemiAnalysis, which alleges manufacturing setbacks have hit the Kyber NVL144 architecture showcased at GTC earlier this year. … SemiAnalysis claims the high-density rack design built for Rubin Ultra GPUs has slipped by more than 12 months. The firm blamed persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e109 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com (beincrypto.com original by Lockridge Okoth); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's delay claims, telling media outlets that its product roadmap remains intact.\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e93 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that a backup plan to bolt two of NVIDIA's current-generation racks together to reach similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra'; SemiAnalysis said this could give Advanced Micro Devices and Google a technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n   \"- e125 [third_party_view; DatacenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028. The Kyber server cabinets, designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via Nvidia's NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027. DatacenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman) reported the claim on 2026-07-06.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported. … The Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e126 [third_party_view; DatacenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture was delayed to 2028 because the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. It also said Nvidia's alternative plan — placing two Oberon racks back-to-back to achieve a similar power output — was scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its 'odd design and heavy operational burden.'\\n    Quote: However, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules together have caused the setback. … “Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,” SemiAnalysis said, adding that Nvidia’s alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e127 [third_party_view; DatacenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said the manufacturing issues affecting Kyber will also impact Nvidia's planned NVL576 system — which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches — meaning that system will likely be delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis said this leaves Nvidia with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.'\\n    Quote: The manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that the system will also likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, the firm noted. … All this leaves Nvidia with “no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,” SemiAnalysis said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e128 [third_party_view; DatacenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] A week before the Kyber report, SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of a two-chiplet design, also due to 'manufacturing execution concerns.' The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\n    Quote: The news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” … The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e169, e175, e187; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e129 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson (reported by DatacenterDynamics); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] In response to a DatacenterDynamics request for comment on the Kyber claims, an Nvidia spokesperson said: 'Our roadmap is intact.'\\n    Quote: In response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e138 [rumour; 24/7 Wall St. (Danielle Liverance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across the company's upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems, according to 24/7 Wall St.; the report does not identify the source of the delay reports, name which Vera Rubin products are affected, or give a new timing.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e167 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis (report on X), relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that NVIDIA's next-generation AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems — specifically that the PCB midplane, a central board connecting all components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects. The delay report was relayed by the-decoder.com.\\n    Quote: Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems. … The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e168 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's planned alternative design called NVL72x2 — which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back — has been scrapped entirely, after cloud providers and large data-center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\\n    Quote: A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e169 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that the more powerful version of NVIDIA's upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has been canceled; only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Quote: The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e128; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e170 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, leaving NVIDIA without a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now — a gap that could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in.\\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e186; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e171 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] According to SemiAnalysis, NVIDIA plans to make up for the shortfall created by the next-generation system setbacks by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\n    Quote: Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"- e172 [rumour; Investing.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Supply-chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 because of manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\\n    Quote: Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e173 [party_statement; Investing.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted the reported product-roadmap delays, saying: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e170 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, leaving NVIDIA without a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now — a gap that could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in.\",\n   \"- e174 [rumour; Investing.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis further alleged in its weekend report that NVIDIA cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients.\\n    Quote: The research firm further alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e175 [rumour; Investing.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis's report pointed to a downsized version of NVIDIA's upcoming Rubin Ultra platform, claiming a crucial four-die configuration had been scrapped.\\n    Quote: Additionally, the report pointed to a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform, claiming a crucial four-die configuration had been scrapped.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e128; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e176 [third_party_view; tikr.com (TIKR); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale server design — built to pack 144 of NVIDIA's most powerful chips into one cabinet and originally slated to launch alongside the next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than a year, from 2027 to 2028. SemiAnalysis attributes the holdup to manufacturing problems with a 