ed it because the design was awkward and the service was expensive to run. SemiAnalysis says this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, which could give rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high en\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e182 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that NVIDIA's next-generation AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties, specifically setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform. NVIDIA did not respond to a request for comment.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform. Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment outside of regular office hours. … Asian technology stocks slumped after a report that Nvidia’s next-generation AI server rack system has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficultie\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e184 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported on X that NVIDIA will not ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet intended for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year; the holdup is attributed to manufacturing challenges with a PCB midplane connecting eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches (the orthogonal backplane).\\n    Quote: Nvidia reportedly won't ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet meant for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year, according to a SemiAnalysis thread on X. The holdup is ostensibly being caused by manufacturing challenges with a PCB midplane that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches, which Nvidia calls the orthogonal backp\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e185 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, after its largest customers balked at running two linked Oberon cabinets as a single unit; NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, and the report says no proven alternative remains to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027. … NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running t\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e186 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's NVL576 configuration, which would tie eight racks together through co-packaged optics, is likely to slip or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures, and that it places a fully production-ready co-packaged optics NVSwitch no earlier than the Feynman generation that follows Rubin, leaving copper as the only near-term option for linking Rubin Ultra at rack scale.\\n    Quote: NVL576, a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics, is likely to slip too or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures. … SemiAnalysis has also placed a fully production-ready co-packaged optics NVSwitch no earlier than the Feynman generation that follows Rubin, which leaves copper as the only near-term solution for linking Rubin Ultra at rack sca\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e170; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e187 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), citing SemiAnalysis context; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] According to the report, NVIDIA dropped its quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part 'last week' over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\\n    Quote: Nvidia dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part last week over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e128; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e188 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Tom's Hardware (Luke James) ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom's Hardware reported that the Kyber delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack, and that NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\\n    Quote: The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n   \"- e189 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA representative responded to Tom's Hardware with the short statement 'Our roadmap is intact,' providing no further details and leaving it unclear whether the statement refers to NVIDIA's original roadmap, planned delivery schedules and hardware, or to an already-updated roadmap.\\n    Quote: An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions. As such, it is unclear if Nvidia's statement refers to its original roadmap, planned delivery schedules, and hardware, or if those particulars had already been changed and the statement refers to a newly updated roadmap.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e91 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post published Monday that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, which is designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; the larger NVL576 system linking eight racks via optical connections is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the syst\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e107, e125, e126, e167, e172, e176, e182, e184, e203, e219; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e92 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-0