{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/third-reader-packet.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n c=x[\\\"control\\\"]\n if c not in [5,8,10,14,15,16,19]: continue\n print(\\\"CONTROL\\\",c)\n pat={5:r\\\"650 billion|650bn|650 billion|1 trillion|\\\\\"'$650\",16:r\"650 billion|650bn|1 trillion|'\"\\\\\"'$650\",8:r\"accelerat|100 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$100|392 billion|554 billion|253 billion\",15:r\"accelerat|100 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$100\",19:r\"accelerat|100 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$100\",10:r\"Oberon|not part of|unaffected\",14:r\"Oberon|not part of|unaffected\"}[c]\n for e in x[\"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\"]:\n  if re.search(pat,e,re.I): print(e[:1600])'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"CONTROL 5\n- e27 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Justin Pope; original source Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang expects $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\n- e74 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis forecasts cumulative AI capital expenditure from 2024 through 2029 will reach about $11.1 trillion and says credit markets will be its main funding source.\n    Quote: Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\n- e27 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Justin Pope; original source Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang expects $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e252; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e252 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear customer commitments.\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e390 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] The article reports Morgan Stanley's forecast that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure and add 19.5 GW of compute capacity in calendar 2027; they added roughly 6.7 GW in 2025, while Google alone is forecast to add 6.8 GW in 2027.\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e173; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nCONTROL 8\n- e236 [fact; FEV Group; original source FEV Group and Microsoft 2026-07-09; from gnews:c8324bfbfe7f0c60c826e72f 2026-07-09] FEV is collaborating with Microsoft to integrate in-vehicle generative AI built on NVIDIA GPU-accelerated compute and AI model microservices; no scale or completion date was given.\n    Quote: FEV is collaborating with Microsoft to integrate powerful, in-vehicle Generative AI capabilities built on NVIDIA GPU-accelerated compute and AI model microservices.\n- e239 [third_party_view; Ornn co-founder and CTO Wayne Nelms, quoted by TechCrunch author Russell Brandom; original source Wayne Nelms ; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] Ornn co-founder and CTO Wayne Nelms said more GPU and accelerator players are entering the market and predicted that current market conditions would broadly persist absent an HBM breakthrough, a supply-demand shift, or a new memory-market entrant.\n    Quote: “More GPU and accelerator players are entering the market.\n- e251 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management guided to accelerating data center segment revenue growth in Q2 FY2027.\n    Quote: Moreover, management guided for further revenue growth acceleration for the second quarter.\n- e253 [third_party_view; Rick Munarriz; original source Rick Munarriz 2026-07-09; from gnews:b412380d12c5a222545d42f1 2026-07-09] The author says Nvidia's revenue growth accelerated for three consecutive quarters.\n    Quote: Nvidia's revenue has accelerated for three consecutive quarters.\n- e304 [fact; TIKR.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] Nvidia reported total revenue of $82 billion, up 85% year over year, in the quarter; this was its third consecutive quarter of accelerating growth.\n    Quote: The company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, marking its third straight quarter of accelerating growth.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e299; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e379 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja; original source Broadcom; CEO Hock Tan ; from gnews:b68c0195283